<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="ID-notes">
  <title>Module 6: Invertibility and Determinants</title>

<objectives>
<p>
In this module, we will discuss how to:
</p>

<ul>
<li>use Gaussian Elimination to determine whether a matrix is invertible,</li>
<li>find the inverse of an invertible matrix,</li>
<li>compute the determinant of a given matrix,</li>
<li>use the determinant to determine whether a matrix is invertible,</li>
<li>analyze the determinant of a product and inverses of matrices.</li>
</ul>
</objectives>

<introduction>
<p>
For more details on this material, see
<url href="https://ximera.osu.edu/oerlinalg/LinearAlgebra/MAT-0050/main">Linear Algebra: An Interactive Introduction (Invertibility Section)</url> and
<url href="https://ximera.osu.edu/oerlinalg/LinearAlgebra/XLAChapter_det/main">Linear Algebra: An Interactive Introduction (Determinants Chapter)</url> and.
</p>
</introduction>

<subsection xml:id="invertibility">
<title>Invertibility of a Matrix</title>

<p>
A square matrix <m>A</m> (size <m>n\times n</m>) is
<term>invertible</term> or <term>nonsingular</term> if there exists an
<m>n\times n</m> matrix <m>C</m> such that
</p>

<me>
CA = I_n \quad\text{and}\quad AC = I_n.
</me>

<p>
That matrix is unique and is denoted <m>A^{-1}</m>. If no such matrix
exists, then <m>A</m> is <term>singular</term>.
</p>

<example>
<title>The 2 × 2 Case</title>

<p>
If
</p>

<me>
A=
\begin{bmatrix}
a   &amp;   b\\
c   &amp;   d
\end{bmatrix}
</me>

<p>
and <m>ad-bc\neq 0</m>, then
</p>

<me>
A^{-1}
=
\frac{1}{ad-bc}
\begin{bmatrix}
d   &amp;   -b\\
-c   &amp;   a
\end{bmatrix}.
</me>
</example>

<example>
<title>Finding an Inverse</title>

<p>Find the inverse of</p>

<me>
B=
\begin{bmatrix}
3   &amp;   1\\
4   &amp;   2
\end{bmatrix}.
</me>

<solution>
<p>
Since
</p>

<me>
\det(B)=3\cdot2-1\cdot4=2\neq0,
</me>

<p>
the matrix is invertible and
</p>

<me>
B^{-1}
=
\frac12
\begin{bmatrix}
2   &amp;   -1\\
-4   &amp;   3
\end{bmatrix}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
1   &amp;   -\frac12\\
-2   &amp;   \frac32
\end{bmatrix}.
</me>

<p>
A quick verification gives
</p>

<me>
BB^{-1}=I_2.
</me>
</solution>
</example>
</subsection>
<subsection xml:id="inverse-properties">
<title>Properties of Inverse Matrices</title>

<subsubsection xml:id="inverse-of-inverse">
<title>The Inverse of an Inverse</title>

<theorem>
<statement>
<p>
If <m>A</m> is invertible, then
</p>

<me>
(A^{-1})^{-1}=A.
</me>
</statement>

<proof>
<p>
Since
</p>

<me>
A^{-1}A=I
\quad\text{and}\quad
AA^{-1}=I,
</me>

<p>
the matrix <m>A</m> acts as the inverse of <m>A^{-1}</m>. Hence
</p>

<me>
(A^{-1})^{-1}=A.
</me>
</proof>
</theorem>

</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="inverse-product">
<title>Inverse of a Product</title>

<theorem>
<statement>
<me>
(AB)^{-1}=B^{-1}A^{-1}.
</me>
</statement>

<proof>

<me>
(AB)(B^{-1}A^{-1})
=
A(BB^{-1})A^{-1}
=
AA^{-1}
=
I.
</me>

<me>
(B^{-1}A^{-1})(AB)
=
B^{-1}(A^{-1}A)B
=
B^{-1}B
=
I.
</me>

<p>
Therefore
</p>

<me>
(AB)^{-1}=B^{-1}A^{-1}.
</me>

</proof>
</theorem>

</subsubsection>

</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="inverse-augmented-matrices">
<title>Computing <m>A^{-1}</m> via Augmented Matrices</title>

<p>
<alert>Method.</alert>
Construct <m>\bigl[A \mid I\bigr]</m> and row-reduce:
</p>

<me>
\left[A \mid I\right]
\xrightarrow{\text{row reduce}}
\left[I \mid A^{-1}\right].
</me>

<p>
If you cannot reach <m>I</m> on the left, then <m>A</m> is not invertible.
</p>

<p>
<alert>Note.</alert>
For <m>2\times 2</m> matrices, the closed-form formula is often faster,
but the augmented matrix method works for square matrices of any size.
</p>

<example xml:id="inverse-3x3-example">
<title>Example: Finding the Inverse of a 3 × 3 Matrix</title>

<p>
We now compute the inverse of a <m>3\times 3</m> matrix using the
augmented matrix method.
</p>

<p>
Let
</p>

<me>
A=
\begin{bmatrix}
2   &amp;   1   &amp;   3\\
1   &amp;   0   &amp;   2\\
3   &amp;   1   &amp;   4
\end{bmatrix}.
</me>

<p>
To find <m>A^{-1}</m> (if it exists), we row-reduce the augmented matrix
<m>\bigl[A\mid I_3\bigr]</m>:
</p>

<me>
\left[
\begin{array}{ccc|ccc}
2   &amp;   1   &amp;   3   &amp;   1   &amp;   0   &amp;   0\\
1   &amp;   0   &amp;   2   &amp;   0   &amp;   1   &amp;   0\\
3   &amp;   1   &amp;   4   &amp;   0   &amp;   0   &amp;   1
\end{array}
\right].
</me>

<p>
<alert>Step 1: Make a pivot in row 1 (swap <m>R_1</m> and <m>R_2</m>).</alert>
</p>

<me>
\left[
\begin{array}{ccc|ccc}
1   &amp;   0   &amp;   2   &amp;   0   &amp;   1   &amp;   0\\
2   &amp;   1   &amp;   3   &amp;   1   &amp;   0   &amp;   0\\
3   &amp;   1   &amp;   4   &amp;   0   &amp;   0   &amp;   1
\end{array}
\right].
</me>

<p>
<alert>
Step 2: Zero out below pivot 1 using
<m>R_2\gets R_2-2R_1</m> and
<m>R_3\gets R_3-3R_1</m>.
</alert>
</p>

<me>
\left[
\begin{array}{ccc|ccc}
1   &amp;   0   &amp;   2   &amp;   0   &amp;   1   &amp;   0\\
0   &amp;   1   &amp;   -1   &amp;   1   &amp;   -2   &amp;   0\\
0   &amp;   1   &amp;   -2   &amp;   0   &amp;   -3   &amp;   1
\end{array}
\right].
</me>

<p>
<alert>
Step 3: Fix the pivot in row 2 and eliminate below it:
<m>R_3\gets R_3-R_2</m>.
</alert>
</p>

<me>
\left[
\begin{array}{ccc|ccc}
1   &amp;   0   &amp;   2   &amp;   0   &amp;   1   &amp;   0\\
0   &amp;   1   &amp;   -1   &amp;   1   &amp;   -2   &amp;   0\\
0   &amp;   0   &amp;   -1   &amp;   -1   &amp;   -1   &amp;   1
\end{array}
\right].
</me>

<p>
<alert>
Step 4: Make the pivot in row 3 equal to 1 by
<m>R_3\gets -R_3</m>.
</alert>
</p>

<me>
\left[
\begin{array}{ccc|ccc}
1   &amp;   0   &amp;   2   &amp;   0   &amp;   1   &amp;   0\\
0   &amp;   1   &amp;   -1   &amp;   1   &amp;   -2   &amp;   0\\
0   &amp;   0   &amp;   1   &amp;   1   &amp;   1   &amp;   -1
\end{array}
\right].
</me>

<p>
<alert>Step 5: Clear above the pivot in column 3.</alert>
</p>

<p>
Use
<m>R_1\gets R_1-2R_3</m>
and
<m>R_2\gets R_2+R_3</m>.
</p>

<me>
\left[
\begin{array}{ccc|ccc}
1   &amp;   0   &amp;   0   &amp;   -2   &amp;   -1   &amp;   2\\
0   &amp;   1   &amp;   0   &amp;   2   &amp;   -1   &amp;   -1\\
0   &amp;   0   &amp;   1   &amp;   1   &amp;   1   &amp;   -1
\end{array}
\right].
</me>

<p>
The left block is now <m>I_3</m>, so the right block is
<m>A^{-1}</m>.
</p>

<me>
A^{-1}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
-2   &amp;   -1   &amp;   2\\
2   &amp;   -1   &amp;   -1\\
1   &amp;   1   &amp;   -1
\end{bmatrix}.
</me>

<p>
<alert>Check your work.</alert>
A quick check confirms
</p>

<me>
AA^{-1}=I_3.
</me>

<p>
Thus <m>A</m> is invertible, and the computation is correct.
</p>

</example>

</subsection>
<subsection xml:id="determinants">
<title>Determinants</title>

<introduction>
<p>
<alert>Concept.</alert>
The determinant is a single number attached to a square matrix that
(among many roles) measures how the associated linear map scales
<em>area</em> (in <m>\mathbb{R}^2</m>) or <em>volume</em>
(in <m>\mathbb{R}^3</m>). If <m>\det(A)=0</m>, then
<m>A</m> flattens some dimension and is not invertible.
</p>
</introduction>

<subsubsection xml:id="computing-determinants">
<title>How to Compute Determinants</title>

<p>
<alert>For <m>2\times2</m>:</alert>
</p>

<me>
\det
\begin{bmatrix}
a  &amp;  b\\
c  &amp;  d
\end{bmatrix}
=
ad-bc.
</me>

<p>
<alert>Cofactor expansion (Laplace).</alert>
For <m>A=[a_{ij}]_{n\times n}</m> and
<m>n\ge 2</m>,
</p>

<me>
\det(A)
=
\sum_{j=1}^{n}
(-1)^{1+j}
a_{1j}\det(A_{1j}),
</me>

<p>
where <m>A_{1j}</m> is the matrix obtained by deleting row
<m>1</m> and column <m>j</m>.
</p>

<p>
<alert>Cofactor expansion diagram (first row).</alert>
</p>

<image>
  <shortdescription>Diagram illustrating cofactor expansion by selecting the first row and its complementary minor.</shortdescription>
    <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.1]

\draw [fill=lightgray!30] (0.1,2.1) rectangle (2.9,2.9);
\draw [fill=blue!5] (1.1,0.1) rectangle (2.9,1.9);
\draw (1,0) -- (1,3);
\draw (2,0) -- (2,3);
\draw (0,1) -- (3,1);
\draw (0,2) -- (3,2);
\draw (0,0) rectangle (3,3);
\node at (0.5,2.5) {$a_{11}$};
\node at (1.5,2.5) {$a_{12}$};
\node at (2.5,2.5) {$a_{13}$};
\node at (0.5,1.5) {$a_{21}$};
\node at (1.5,1.5) {$a_{22}$};
\node at (2.5,1.5) {$a_{23}$};
\node at (0.5,0.5) {$a_{31}$};
\node at (1.5,0.5) {$a_{32}$};
\node at (2.5,0.5) {$a_{33}$};
\draw[red,thick] (-0.1,2.5) -- (3.1,2.5);
\draw[red,thick] (0.5,-0.1) -- (0.5,3.1);
\draw [->] (3.2,1) -- (3.8,1);
\draw (4,0) rectangle (6,2);
\draw [fill=blue!5] (4.1,0.1) rectangle (5.9,1.9);
\draw (5,0) -- (5,2);
\draw (4,1) -- (6,1);
\node at (5,2.5) {$A_{11}$};
\node at (4.5,1.5) {$a_{22}$};
\node at (5.5,1.5) {$a_{23}$};
\node at (4.5,0.5) {$a_{32}$};
\node at (5.5,0.5) {$a_{33}$};
\end{tikzpicture}
    </latex-image>

</image>

<example>
<title>Cofactor Expansion Example</title>

<md>
\det
\begin{bmatrix}
1 &amp; 3 &amp; 0\\
5 &amp; 0 &amp; 2\\
2 &amp; 1 &amp; 1
\end{bmatrix}
</md>
<md>
=
1\cdot
\det
\begin{bmatrix}
0 &amp; 2\\
1 &amp; 1
\end{bmatrix}
-
3\cdot
\det
\begin{bmatrix}
5 &amp; 2\\
2 &amp; 1
\end{bmatrix}
+
0\cdot(\cdots)
</md>
<md>
=
1(0\cdot1-2\cdot1)
-
3(5\cdot1-2\cdot2)
</md>
<md>
=
-2-3(1)
=
-5.
</md>

</example>

<theorem>
<title>Cofactor Expansion Along Any Row or Column</title>

<p>
<em>Row expansion:</em>
For any fixed row <m>i</m>,
</p>

<me>
\det(A)
=
\sum_{j=1}^{n}
a_{ij}C_{ij}.
</me>

<p>
<em>Column expansion:</em>
For any fixed column <m>j</m>,
</p>

<me>
\det(A)
=
\sum_{i=1}^{n}
a_{ij}C_{ij}.
</me>

</theorem>

<example>
<title>Determinant of a 4 × 4 Matrix</title>

<p>
Find the determinant of
</p>

<me>
A=
\begin{bmatrix}
3  &amp;  7  &amp;  1  &amp;  1\\
0  &amp;  2  &amp;  2  &amp;  2\\
0  &amp;  0  &amp;  3  &amp;  2\\
0  &amp;  0  &amp;  1  &amp;  1
\end{bmatrix}.
</me>

<p>
Using cofactor expansion along the first row:
</p>

<me>
\det(A)
=
3\det(A_{11})
-
7\det(A_{12})
+
\det(A_{13})
-
\det(A_{14}).
</me>

<p>
Compute each cofactor.
</p>

<p>
The submatrix <m>A_{11}</m> is
</p>

<me>
\begin{bmatrix}
2  &amp;  2  &amp;  2\\
0  &amp;  3  &amp;  2\\
0  &amp;  1  &amp;  1
\end{bmatrix},
\qquad
C_{11}=(+1)\det(A_{11}).
</me>

<p>
The submatrix <m>A_{12}</m> is
</p>

<me>
\begin{bmatrix}
0  &amp;  2  &amp;  2\\
0  &amp;  3  &amp;  2\\
0  &amp;  1  &amp;  1
\end{bmatrix},
\qquad
C_{12}=(-1)\det(A_{12}).
</me>

<p>
The submatrix <m>A_{13}</m> is
</p>

<me>
\begin{bmatrix}
0  &amp;  2  &amp;  2\\
0  &amp;  0  &amp;  2\\
0  &amp;  0  &amp;  1
\end{bmatrix},
\qquad
C_{13}=(+1)\det(A_{13}).
</me>

<p>
The submatrix <m>A_{14}</m> is
</p>

<me>
\begin{bmatrix}
0  &amp;  2  &amp;  2\\
0  &amp;  0  &amp;  3\\
0  &amp;  0  &amp;  1
\end{bmatrix},
\qquad
C_{14}=(-1)\det(A_{14}).
</me>

<p>
Now compute the determinants of the
<m>3\times3</m> matrices:
</p>

<me>
\det(A_{11})
=
2
\det
\begin{bmatrix}
3  &amp;  2\\
1  &amp;  1
\end{bmatrix}
-
2
\det
\begin{bmatrix}
0  &amp;  2\\
0  &amp;  1
\end{bmatrix}
+
2
\det
\begin{bmatrix}
0  &amp;  3\\
0  &amp;  1
\end{bmatrix}</me><me>
=
2(3\cdot1-2\cdot1).
</me>

<me>
\det(A_{11})=2.
</me>

<me>
\det(A_{12})
=
0\cdot
\det
\begin{bmatrix}
3  &amp;  2\\
1  &amp;  1
\end{bmatrix}
-
2\cdot
\det
\begin{bmatrix}
0  &amp;  2\\
0  &amp;  1
\end{bmatrix}
+
2\cdot
\det
\begin{bmatrix}
0  &amp;  3\\
0  &amp;  1
\end{bmatrix}
=
0.
</me>

<me>
\det(A_{13})
=
0\cdot
\det
\begin{bmatrix}
0  &amp;  2\\
0  &amp;  1
\end{bmatrix}
-
2\cdot
\det
\begin{bmatrix}
0  &amp;  2\\
0  &amp;  1
\end{bmatrix}
+
2\cdot
\det
\begin{bmatrix}
0  &amp;  0\\
0  &amp;  0
\end{bmatrix}
=
0.
</me>

<me>
\det(A_{14})
=
0\cdot
\det
\begin{bmatrix}
0  &amp;  3\\
0  &amp;  1
\end{bmatrix}
-
2\cdot
\det
\begin{bmatrix}
0  &amp;  3\\
0  &amp;  1
\end{bmatrix}
+
2\cdot
\det
\begin{bmatrix}
0  &amp;  0\\
0  &amp;  0
\end{bmatrix}
=
0.
</me>

<p>
Thus the full cofactor expansion gives
</p>

<me>
\det(A)
=
3(2)-7(0)+1(0)-1(0)
=
6.
</me>

<p>
As an alternative, let us use the first column expansion:
</p>

<me>
\det(A)
=
\det
\begin{bmatrix}
3  &amp;  7  &amp;  1  &amp;  1\\
0  &amp;  2  &amp;  2  &amp;  2\\
0  &amp;  0  &amp;  3  &amp;  2\\
0  &amp;  0  &amp;  1  &amp;  1
\end{bmatrix}
=
3\det
\begin{bmatrix}
2  &amp;  2  &amp;  2\\
0  &amp;  3  &amp;  2\\
0  &amp;  1  &amp;  1
\end{bmatrix}
+0+0+0</me><me>
=
3\cdot2
\det
\begin{bmatrix}
3  &amp;  2\\
1  &amp;  1
\end{bmatrix}
=
3\cdot2\cdot1
=
6.
</me>

</example>

<p>
<alert>Row-operations and determinants.</alert>
</p>

<ul>
<li>
Add a multiple of one row to another:
the determinant is <em>unchanged</em>.
</li>

<li>
Swap two rows:
the determinant changes sign.
</li>

<li>
Multiply a row by <m>k</m>:
the determinant is multiplied by <m>k</m>.
</li>
</ul>

<p>
<alert>Row-operation diagrams (applied to a 2 × 2 matrix).</alert>
</p>

<p>
<em>Add a multiple of one row:</em>
<m>\mathrm{Row}_2\to\mathrm{Row}_2+k\mathrm{Row}_1</m>
keeps the determinant unchanged.
<me>\begin{vmatrix} 1&amp;2\\3&amp; 4\end{vmatrix}=\begin{vmatrix}1+5\cdot3&amp;2+5\cdot4\\3&amp;4\end{vmatrix}</me>
</p>

<p>
<em>Swap two rows:</em>
the sign of the determinant flips.
<me>\begin{vmatrix} 1&amp;2\\3&amp; 4\end{vmatrix}=-\begin{vmatrix}3&amp; 4\\1&amp;2\end{vmatrix}</me>
</p>


<p>
<em>Scale one row:</em>
the determinant scales by the same factor.
<me>\begin{vmatrix} 5&amp;10\\3&amp; 4\end{vmatrix}=5\cdot \begin{vmatrix}1&amp;2\\3&amp;4\end{vmatrix}</me>

</p>


</subsubsection>

</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="determinant-invertibility-rules">
<title>Determinant and Invertibility  and  Product/Inverse Rules</title>

<p>
<alert>Invertibility test.</alert>
A square matrix <m>A</m> is invertible if and only if
<m>\det(A)\neq 0</m>.
</p>

<p>
<alert>Useful properties.</alert>
</p>

<ul>
<li><m>\det(A^\top)=\det(A)</m>.</li>

<li>
If <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> are
<m>n\times n</m> matrices, then
<m>\det(AB)=\det(A)\det(B)</m>.
</li>

<li>
If <m>A</m> is invertible, then
<m>\det(A^{-1})=\dfrac{1}{\det(A)}</m>.
</li>

<li>
If <m>A</m> is triangular, then
<m>\det(A)</m> is the product of its diagonal entries.
</li>
</ul>

</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="inverse-matrix-theorem">
<title>Characterization of Invertible Matrices (Equivalences)</title>

<p>
For an <m>n\times n</m> matrix <m>A</m>, the following twelve
statements are all <alert>equivalent</alert>.
This collection is sometimes called the
<em>Inverse Matrix Theorem</em>.
Any one of these being true guarantees all the others.
</p>

<ol label="(1)">

<li><m>A</m> is invertible.</li>

<li><m>A</m> is row equivalent to the identity matrix <m>I_n</m>.</li>

<li><m>A</m> has <m>n</m> pivot positions (one in each row and each column).</li>

<li>
The equation <m>A\mathbf{x}=\mathbf{0}</m>
has only the trivial solution
<m>\mathbf{x}=\mathbf{0}</m>.
</li>

<li>The columns of <m>A</m> are linearly independent.</li>

<li>
The linear transformation
<m>T(\mathbf{x})=A\mathbf{x}</m>
is one-to-one.
</li>

<li>
The equation
<m>A\mathbf{x}=\mathbf{b}</m>
has at least one solution for every
<m>\mathbf{b}\in\mathbb{R}^n</m>.
</li>

<li>The columns of <m>A</m> span <m>\mathbb{R}^n</m>.</li>

<li>
The linear transformation
<m>T(\mathbf{x})=A\mathbf{x}</m>
maps <m>\mathbb{R}^n</m> onto <m>\mathbb{R}^n</m>.
</li>

<li>
There exists a matrix <m>C</m> such that
<m>CA=I_n</m>
(that is, <m>A</m> has a left inverse).
</li>

<li>
There exists a matrix <m>D</m> such that
<m>AD=I_n</m>
(that is, <m>A</m> has a right inverse).
</li>

<li><m>A^\top</m> is invertible.</li>

</ol>

<p>
<alert>Interpretation.</alert>
These twelve statements connect algebra, geometry, and linear
transformations:
</p>

<ul>
<li>(1)–(3) are row-operation and pivot statements.</li>

<li>(4)–(6) relate to one-to-one behavior.</li>

<li>(7)–(9) relate to onto behavior and existence of solutions.</li>

<li>(10)–(12) describe left and right inverses and invertibility of the transpose.</li>
</ul>

<p>
Together, they provide many equivalent ways to detect invertibility.
</p>

</subsection>
</section>